<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-counting-squares" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Counting Squares</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p component="core">
                        2.2 Area Approximations
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        2.1 Area Approximations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p component="core">
                        Estimate area of irregular shapes by counting squares technique
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        Use counting squares technique to estimate area of irregular shapes and curves
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        2 lessons of 40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Estimating the area of irregular shapes by counting full and partial squares on a grid.
         Suggested activity: Learners trace a leaf or the outline of the school compound from a map onto squared paper and estimate its area by counting squares.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Irregular shape over a grid with a slider for grid size; the count of full and partial squares updates, showing the estimate improving as squares get smaller. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core (a), Essential (a). Lessons 1.1 and 1.2 as subsections.
         Activity: as planning notes (a traced leaf; the school compound is an exercise) | changed: lesson 1.2 adds a plot under a curve (Essential names curves)
         JSXGraph: fig-counting-squares-interactive (assets/jsxgraph/counting-squares-grid.js)
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/07-area-approximations/01-counting-squares/ -->

    <introduction>
        <question xml:id="kiq-area-approximations">
            <p><em>Where do we need to find areas of shapes with curved or irregular edges?</em></p>
        </question>
        <p>
            Few pieces of land in Kenya are perfect rectangles. A shamba may have a river along one side, a plot may follow a winding road, and the lakes and forests on a county map have edges that no formula describes. Water engineers need the area of a river's cross-section to know how much water it carries to an intake or under a bridge.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter gives three ways to estimate such areas. Counting squares works for any outline drawn on a grid. The trapezoidal rule replaces narrow strips of the region by trapezia. The mid-ordinate rule replaces them by rectangles whose heights are measured at the middle of each strip. As you work through the chapter, look out for places in your county where these methods would help.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 1.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-counting-squares-for-irregular-shapes">
        <title>Counting Squares for Irregular Shapes</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> what full squares and part squares are; why a part square counts as a half; that a square on a map stands for a square on the ground.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> count full and part squares, estimate an area as full squares <m>+ \tfrac{1}{2} \times</m> part squares, and convert with the size of a square or a map scale.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> estimate the areas of ponds, forests and plots from plans and maps.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners estimate the area of a leaf by counting squares and see that smaller squares give a steadier answer.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Each learner traces a leaf on 1 cm squared paper; groups without leaves use <xref ref="fig-leaf-on-grid"/>. Collect five estimates on the board before part (d). Do not give the rule until learners have argued about the part squares.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-tracing-a-leaf">
            <title>Tracing a Leaf</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    On a walk in Kakamega Forest, Nekesa picked a leaf and traced round it on 1 cm squared paper.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-leaf-on-grid">
                    <caption>Nekesa's leaf on 1 cm squares.</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
