                                </image>
                            </figure>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>78</m> full and about <m>50</m> part squares: <m>(78 + 25) \times 0.25 = 25.75\,\text{cm}^2</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The true area of the leaf is <m>25.6\,\text{cm}^2</m>. Which grid gave the closer estimate? Use the interactive in <xref ref="fig-counting-squares-interactive"/> to try other grid sizes.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>For this leaf the 1 cm grid happens to be slightly closer (<m>0.1\,\text{cm}^2</m> out, against <m>0.15\,\text{cm}^2</m>): for one shape, luck plays a part. The interactive shows the trend: 2 cm squares give <m>28\,\text{cm}^2</m>, then <m>25.5</m>, <m>25.75</m> and <m>25.75\,\text{cm}^2</m>. Once the squares are small the estimate settles close to the true area and no longer jumps about.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise the counting rule, the area of one square, and why finer grids help.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> use two learners' counts for their own leaves to name full squares and part squares.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Each part square counts as a half, because on average a part square is about half covered.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The estimate in squares is multiplied by the area of one square: <m>1\,\text{cm}^2</m> on 1 cm paper, <m>0.25\,\text{cm}^2</m> on 0.5 cm paper.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Smaller squares give a better estimate; show the interactive at each grid size.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-counting-squares">
            <title>Counting Squares</title>
            <p>
                Draw the shape on a grid of squares. A <term>full square</term> lies wholly inside the shape. A <term>part square</term> is any other square that the edge of the shape passes through, however little of the shape is inside it. If there are <m>F</m> full squares and <m>P</m> part squares, then
                <md>\text{estimate} = F + \tfrac{1}{2}P \text{ squares}</md>
                because a part square is, on average, about half covered. Multiply the number of squares by the area that one square stands for to get the area.
            </p>
            <p>
                Smaller squares usually give a better estimate: the part squares cover a smaller share of the shape, and each one can be wrong by less.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <note xml:id="note-another-way-to-count">
            <title>Another Way to Count</title>
            <p>
                Some people count a part square as <m>1</m> if at least half of it is inside the shape and as <m>0</m> otherwise. The two ways usually give similar estimates. This book uses <m>F + \tfrac{1}{2}P</m>, because it needs no judgement about how full each square is.
            </p>
        </note>

        <figure xml:id="fig-counting-squares-interactive">
            <caption>Drag the slider to change the size of the squares. Full squares are shaded and part squares have a dotted outline; the counts and the estimate update. Compare the estimate with the true area, <m>25.6\,\text{cm}^2</m>.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-counting-squares-grid" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/counting-squares-grid.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-counting-squares-grid" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>The leaf is the one in <xref ref="act-tracing-a-leaf"/>. Drag the <alert>grid size</alert> slider from 2 cm to 0.25 cm.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Read the estimate at each size: you should see <m>28</m>, <m>25.5</m>, <m>25.75</m> and <m>25.75\,\text{cm}^2</m>. Which sizes give estimates close to <m>25.6\,\text{cm}^2</m>?</p></li>
                            <li><p>At 0.25 cm, how many part squares are there compared with full squares? Why does that make the estimate more reliable?</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-fish-pond">
            <title>A Fish Pond in Kirinyaga</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A farmer in Kirinyaga draws the plan of her fish pond on a grid. Each square stands for <m>2\,\text{m}</m> by <m>2\,\text{m}</m>. Estimate the area of the pond.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-pond-on-grid">
                    <caption>The fish pond on squares of <m>2\,\text{m}</m> by <m>2\,\text{m}</m>. Full squares are shaded; part squares are dotted.</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <shortdescription>A pond outline on a grid of 12 by 9 squares: 18 squares wholly inside are shaded and 26 squares cut by the edge are dotted. Each square is 2 m by 2 m.</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Full squares: 18. Part squares: 26. Each square stands for 2 m by 2 m.</p></description>
