                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>There are <m>F = 18</m> full squares and <m>P = 26</m> part squares, so
                    <md>\text{estimate} = 18 + \tfrac{1}{2} \times 26 = 18 + 13 = 31 \text{ squares}</md>
                    One square stands for <m>2 \times 2 = 4\,\text{m}^2</m>, so the area of the pond is about <m>31 \times 4 = 124\,\text{m}^2</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-forest-on-a-map">
            <title>A Forest on a Map</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A forest block near Kakamega is drawn on a map of scale <m>1 : 40\,000</m> with a grid of 1 cm squares. Its outline covers <m>18</m> full squares and <m>14</m> part squares. Estimate the area of the forest in hectares (<m>1 \text{ hectare} = 10\,000\,\text{m}^2</m>).</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p><alert>Step 1:</alert> the estimate in squares is <m>18 + \tfrac{1}{2} \times 14 = 18 + 7 = 25</m> squares.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 2:</alert> at a scale of <m>1 : 40\,000</m>, <m>1\,\text{cm}</m> on the map stands for <m>40\,000\,\text{cm}</m> <m>= 400\,\text{m}</m> on the ground. So one square on the map stands for a square of side <m>400\,\text{m}</m>:
                    <md>400 \times 400 = 160\,000\,\text{m}^2 = 16 \text{ hectares}</md></p>
                <p><alert>Step 3:</alert> the area is about <m>25 \times 16 = 400</m> hectares.</p>
                <p>A square on the map stands for a square on the ground, so square the real length of its side. Multiplying the <m>25</m> squares by <m>400</m> alone would give a length, not an area.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-two-grids-one-reservoir">
            <title>Two Grids, One Reservoir</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A reservoir is drawn on a county map of scale <m>1 : 200\,000</m>. On a grid of 1 cm squares it covers <m>12</m> full and <m>16</m> part squares. On a grid of 0.5 cm squares it covers <m>62</m> full and <m>34</m> part squares. Find both estimates of its area in square kilometres. Which is more reliable?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>At <m>1 : 200\,000</m>, <m>1\,\text{cm}</m> on the map stands for <m>200\,000\,\text{cm}</m> <m>= 2\,\text{km}</m>, so a 1 cm square stands for <m>2 \times 2 = 4\,\text{km}^2</m>.</p>
                <p>1 cm grid: <m>12 + \tfrac{1}{2} \times 16 = 20</m> squares, so about <m>20 \times 4 = 80\,\text{km}^2</m>.</p>
                <p>0.5 cm grid: a small square stands for <m>1\,\text{km}</m> by <m>1\,\text{km}</m>, that is <m>1\,\text{km}^2</m>. The estimate is <m>62 + \tfrac{1}{2} \times 34 = 79</m> small squares, so about <m>79\,\text{km}^2</m>.</p>
                <p>The two estimates agree to within about <m>1\,\text{km}^2</m>. The finer count is more reliable, because its part squares are smaller.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>37.6</c>. Give exact answers unless the question asks you to round.</p>

