                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </statement>
                <answer><p>About <m>29\,\text{cm}^2</m> (<m>17</m> full and about <m>24</m> part squares).</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>F = 17</m> and <m>P = 24</m> (a count within one or two squares of these is fine), so the estimate is <m>17 + \tfrac{1}{2} \times 24 = 29</m> squares, about <m>29\,\text{cm}^2</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-plot-on-five-metre-squares">
                <statement><p>A plan of a plot in Embu is drawn on squares that stand for <m>5\,\text{m}</m> by <m>5\,\text{m}</m>. The plot covers <m>40</m> full and <m>26</m> part squares. Estimate its area.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>1325\,\text{m}^2</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>40 + \tfrac{1}{2} \times 26 = 53</m> squares; one square is <m>5 \times 5 = 25\,\text{m}^2</m>; area about <m>53 \times 25 = 1325\,\text{m}^2</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-plot-on-a-1-to-2500-map">
                <statement><p>On a map of scale <m>1 : 2500</m> with a grid of 1 cm squares, a shamba covers <m>36</m> full and <m>20</m> part squares. Estimate its area in square metres and in hectares.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>28\,750\,\text{m}^2 = 2.875</m> hectares</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>1\,\text{cm}</m> on the map stands for <m>2500\,\text{cm}</m> <m>= 25\,\text{m}</m>, so one square stands for <m>25 \times 25 = 625\,\text{m}^2</m>. The estimate is <m>36 + 10 = 46</m> squares, so the area is about <m>46 \times 625 = 28\,750\,\text{m}^2</m>, which is <m>28\,750 \div 10\,000 = 2.875</m> hectares.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-how-many-part-squares">
                <statement><p>Using 1 cm squares, Kiprono estimates the area of a shape as <m>60\,\text{cm}^2</m>. He counted <m>48</m> full squares. How many part squares did he count?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>24</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>48 + \tfrac{1}{2}P = 60</m>, so <m>\tfrac{1}{2}P = 12</m> and <m>P = 24</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-our-school-compound">
                <statement><p>Find your school compound on a printed county map or an online map that shows a scale. Trace its outline onto squared paper, count the full and part squares, and use the scale of the map to estimate the area of the compound in square metres and in hectares.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Answers depend on the school.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>Check the scale step: <m>1\,\text{cm}</m> on a map of scale <m>1 : S</m> stands for <m>S \div 100\,\text{m}</m>, so one 1 cm square stands for <m>(S \div 100)^2\,\text{m}^2</m>. Then area <m>\approx (F + \tfrac{1}{2}P) \times (S \div 100)^2\,\text{m}^2</m>, and divide by <m>10\,000</m> for hectares.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-offset-square-challenge">
                <title>Challenge: A Square Off the Grid</title>
                <statement><p>A square of side <m>3.5\,\text{cm}</m> is drawn with opposite corners at <m>(0.25, 0.25)</m> and <m>(3.75, 3.75)</m> on a grid of 1 cm squares. Count the full and part squares, estimate the area, and compare with the true area. Explain the difference.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>How much of each part square is covered?</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>F = 4</m>, <m>P = 12</m>, estimate <m>10\,\text{cm}^2</m>; true area <m>12.25\,\text{cm}^2</m>.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>The squares from <m>1</m> to <m>3</m> in both directions are full: <m>2 \times 2 = 4</m>. The other <m>12</m> squares of the <m>4 \times 4</m> block are part squares. Estimate <m>4 + 6 = 10\,\text{cm}^2</m>; true area <m>3.5^2 = 12.25\,\text{cm}^2</m>. Every part square here is more than half covered (the edge squares are <m>\tfrac{3}{4}</m> covered and the corner squares <m>\tfrac{9}{16}</m>), so counting each as <m>\tfrac{1}{2}</m> gives too little. The <q>half</q> works on average only when the edge cuts squares in all sorts of ways.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-counting-squares-irregular">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a grid with the full squares already shaded, so learners count only the part squares. Work through <xref ref="example-fish-pond"/> and <xref ref="example-forest-on-a-map"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-count-the-blot"/> and <xref ref="ex-plot-on-five-metre-squares"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-count-the-blot"/> to <xref ref="ex-our-school-compound"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-offset-square-challenge"/>. Extension: draw a shape on 1 cm squares for which <m>F + \tfrac{1}{2}P</m> is exact (one answer: a right-angled triangle with legs of <m>4\,\text{cm}</m> along grid lines, so the hypotenuse passes through grid corners; <m>F = 6</m>, <m>P = 4</m>, estimate <m>8 = \tfrac{1}{2} \times 4 \times 4</m>, because every part square is cut exactly in half).</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 1.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-counting-squares-under-a-curve">
        <title>Counting Squares Under a Curve</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> what is meant by a region bounded by a curve, the <m>x</m>-axis and two vertical lines; that the sides of a square may stand for different lengths.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> count squares under a curve, and find the area one square stands for from both scales.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> estimate the areas of plots bounded by a stream and of flower beds, and check the method against shapes whose areas are known.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners estimate the area of a shamba bounded by a road, two fences and a stream, then improve the estimate on a finer grid.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Give out <xref ref="fig-shamba-by-stream"/> and <xref ref="fig-shamba-fine-grid"/> on paper. Half the class counts on the 10 m grid and half on the 5 m grid; then compare. Ask: <q>Why might a surveyor prefer narrow strips?</q> This leads to <xref ref="sec-trapezoidal-rule"/>.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-shamba-by-the-stream">
            <title>A Shamba by the Stream</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A shamba near Nyeri lies between a straight road, two straight fences at right angles to the road, and a stream. The plan shows it on squares of <m>10\,\text{m}</m> by <m>10\,\text{m}</m>.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-shamba-by-stream">
                    <caption>The shamba on squares of <m>10\,\text{m}</m> by <m>10\,\text{m}</m>. The road runs along the bottom and the stream forms the curved edge.</caption>
                    <image width="70%">
                        <shortdescription>A plot between a road along the bottom, two fences and a curved stream, 80 m long, drawn on 10 m squares. The stream is 23 m from the road at each fence and about 39 m from it in the middle.</shortdescription>
