  \node[below, font=\small] at (8,0) {$80$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,0) {$0$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,1) {$10$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,2) {$20$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,3) {$30$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,4) {$40$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,5) {$50$};
  \node[below=12pt] at (4,0) {distance along the road (m)};
  \node[rotate=90] at (-1.3,2.5) {distance from the road (m)};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>How many full squares and how many part squares does the shamba cover?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>22</m> full and <m>8</m> part squares.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Each square is <m>10\,\text{m}</m> by <m>10\,\text{m}</m>. Estimate the area of the shamba in square metres.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>(22 + 4) \times 100 = 2600\,\text{m}^2</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Repeat parts (a) and (b) on the grid of <m>5\,\text{m}</m> squares.</p>
                            <figure xml:id="fig-shamba-fine-grid">
                                <caption>The same shamba on squares of <m>5\,\text{m}</m> by <m>5\,\text{m}</m>.</caption>
                                <image width="70%">
                                    <shortdescription>The same plot between a road, two fences and a curved stream, 80 m long, drawn on 5 m squares.</shortdescription>
