                                    </latex-image>
                                </image>
                            </figure>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>98</m> full and <m>18</m> part squares; <m>(98 + 9) \times 25 = 2675\,\text{m}^2</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>A surveyor's computer gives the area as about <m>2693\,\text{m}^2</m>. Which grid gave the closer estimate, and why?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The 5 m grid (<m>18\,\text{m}^2</m> out, against <m>93\,\text{m}^2</m>): smaller squares follow the stream more closely.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Look at one column of squares on the 10 m grid. Which shape would fit a whole column better than full squares and half squares?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>A strip with a sloping straight top: a trapezium. This prepares <xref ref="sec-trapezoidal-rule"/>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To apply the counting rule to a region under a curve and to find the area of one square from two scales.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> the two halves of the class used the same rule, <m>F + \tfrac{1}{2}P</m>, on different grids; the finer grid was closer.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The area one square stands for is its width times its height in real units. The two may differ, as on a plan drawn with different scales across and up.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A column of squares under a curve is a strip; in the next section each strip becomes a trapezium.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-counting-squares-under-a-curve">
            <title>Counting Squares Under a Curve</title>
            <p>
                The region <term>bounded by</term> the curve <m>y = f(x)</m>, the <m>x</m>-axis and the lines <m>x = a</m> and <m>x = b</m> is the region enclosed by those four edges: it lies above the <m>x</m>-axis, below the curve, and between the two vertical lines.
            </p>
            <p>
                Count full and part squares in it as before: estimate <m>= F + \tfrac{1}{2}P</m> squares. One square stands for (width of a square) <m>\times</m> (height of a square), each in the units on its axis. The two may be different: on a plan with squares <m>5\,\text{m}</m> wide and <m>2\,\text{m}</m> tall, one square stands for <m>5 \times 2 = 10\,\text{m}^2</m>.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-under-a-curve-unit-squares">
            <title>Under a Curve on Unit Squares</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Estimate the area of the region bounded by the curve <m>y = 4.6 + 0.9x - 0.18x^2</m>, the <m>x</m>-axis and the lines <m>x = 0</m> and <m>x = 6</m>, by counting the unit squares in <xref ref="fig-under-curve-unit-squares"/>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-under-curve-unit-squares">
                    <caption>The region under <m>y = 4.6 + 0.9x - 0.18x^2</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 6</m>. Full squares are shaded; part squares are dotted.</caption>
