  \node[below, font=\small] at (0,0) {$0$};
  \node[below, font=\small] at (1,0) {$5$};
  \node[below, font=\small] at (2,0) {$10$};
  \node[below, font=\small] at (3,0) {$15$};
  \node[below, font=\small] at (4,0) {$20$};
  \node[below, font=\small] at (5,0) {$25$};
  \node[below, font=\small] at (6,0) {$30$};
  \node[below, font=\small] at (7,0) {$35$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,0) {$0$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,1) {$2$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,2) {$4$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,3) {$6$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,4) {$8$};
  \node[left, font=\small] at (0,5) {$10$};
  \node[below=12pt] at (3.5,0) {distance along the path (m)};
  \node[rotate=90] at (-1.3,2.5) {distance across (m)};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>There are <m>22</m> full and <m>9</m> part squares, so the estimate is <m>22 + 4.5 = 26.5</m> squares. One square stands for <m>5 \times 2 = 10\,\text{m}^2</m>, so the area is about <m>26.5 \times 10 = 265\,\text{m}^2</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-quarter-circle-check">
            <title>Checking With a Shape We Know</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A quarter circle of radius <m>6.2\,\text{cm}</m> is drawn on 1 cm squares with its centre on a grid corner. Estimate its area by counting squares, and compare with the area from <m>\tfrac{1}{4}\pi r^2</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-quarter-circle-grid">
                    <caption>A quarter circle of radius <m>6.2\,\text{cm}</m> on 1 cm squares. Full squares are shaded; part squares are dotted.</caption>
