  \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (4.384,4.384) node[midway, above left, fill=white, inner sep=1pt, font=\small] {$6.2$ cm};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>There are <m>24</m> full and <m>13</m> part squares, so the estimate is <m>24 + 6.5 = 30.5\,\text{cm}^2</m>.</p>
                <p>The formula gives <m>\tfrac{1}{4} \times \pi \times 6.2^2 = 30.19\,\text{cm}^2</m> (to 2 decimal places). The count is about <m>0.3\,\text{cm}^2</m> too big, about <m>1</m> per cent: counting squares works well for a curved edge.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>37.6</c>. Give exact answers unless the question asks you to round.</p>

