                    </figure>
                </statement>
                <answer><p>About <m>23</m> square units (<m>20</m> full, <m>6</m> part).</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>F = 20</m>, <m>P = 6</m>: estimate <m>20 + 3 = 23</m> square units.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-plot-twenty-by-ten">
                <statement><p>A plan uses squares that stand for <m>20\,\text{m}</m> along a road and <m>10\,\text{m}</m> across it. A plot covers <m>31</m> full and <m>11</m> part squares. Estimate its area in square metres and in hectares.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>7300\,\text{m}^2 = 0.73</m> hectares</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>31 + 5.5 = 36.5</m> squares; one square is <m>20 \times 10 = 200\,\text{m}^2</m>; area about <m>36.5 \times 200 = 7300\,\text{m}^2 = 0.73</m> hectares.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-circle-by-counting">
                <statement><p>A circle of radius <m>5.5\,\text{cm}</m> is drawn with its centre on a grid corner of 1 cm squares. It covers <m>76</m> full and <m>44</m> part squares. Estimate its area, and compare with <m>\pi r^2</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>98\,\text{cm}^2</m>; <m>\pi r^2 = 95.03\,\text{cm}^2</m>.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>Estimate <m>76 + 22 = 98\,\text{cm}^2</m>. <m>\pi \times 5.5^2 = 95.03\,\text{cm}^2</m> (to 2 decimal places). The estimate is about <m>3\,\text{cm}^2</m> too big, about <m>3</m> per cent.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-why-finer-squares">
                <statement><p>Explain, in two sentences, why smaller squares usually give a better estimate of an area.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>With smaller squares the part squares make up a smaller share of the shape, and each part square, counted as a half, can be wrong by at most half of a smaller area.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-squares-of-different-shapes">
                <statement><p>On a plan, each square stands for <m>4\,\text{m}</m> across and <m>3\,\text{m}</m> up. A region covers <m>18</m> full and <m>10</m> part squares. Estimate its area. Why does one square not stand for <m>7\,\text{m}^2</m> or <m>14\,\text{m}^2</m>?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>276\,\text{m}^2</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>18 + 5 = 23</m> squares; one square stands for <m>4 \times 3 = 12\,\text{m}^2</m>; area about <m>23 \times 12 = 276\,\text{m}^2</m>. <m>7</m> is the sum of the two sides and <m>14</m> is the perimeter of a square; area is width times height.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-triangle-counts-exactly">
                <title>Challenge: When Counting Is Exact</title>
                <statement><p>Count the unit squares under the line <m>y = 2x</m> from <m>x = 0</m> to <m>x = 4</m>, and compare the estimate with the area of the triangle. Explain why the estimate is exact here.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Look at the two part squares in each column together.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>F = 12</m>, <m>P = 8</m>, estimate <m>16</m>, the same as <m>\tfrac{1}{2} \times 4 \times 8 = 16</m>.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>In the column from <m>x = c</m> to <m>x = c + 1</m> the line rises from <m>2c</m> to <m>2c + 2</m>, so it passes through two squares. The lower one is <m>\tfrac{3}{4}</m> covered and the upper one <m>\tfrac{1}{4}</m> covered: together they hold <m>1</m> square of area, exactly what <m>2 \times \tfrac{1}{2}</m> counts. Columns: full <m>0, 2, 4, 6</m>, so <m>F = 12</m>; <m>P = 8</m>; estimate <m>12 + 4 = 16</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-counting-squares-under-a-curve">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Number the columns on the grid in advance and work out the area of one square together first. Work through <xref ref="example-under-a-curve-unit-squares"/> and <xref ref="example-flower-bed-nakuru"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-count-under-curve"/> and <xref ref="ex-plot-twenty-by-ten"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-count-under-curve"/> to <xref ref="ex-squares-of-different-shapes"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-triangle-counts-exactly"/>. Extension: find a straight line through <m>(0, 1)</m> for which counting squares over <m>0 \le x \le 4</m> is not exact (one answer: <m>y = 1 + 0.6x</m> gives <m>F = 6</m>, <m>P = 6</m>, estimate <m>9</m>, against the trapezium's area <m>8.8</m>, because the part squares in a column no longer pair to make <m>1</m>).</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

